+### Pseudo-depth Feature Rendering
+- **Definition**: A method to render features for a 3D scene representation (InstantNGP) by computing a pseudo-depth surface and sampling features locally around it, avoiding full volume rendering for computational efficiency [S3].
+- **Step**: Compute the pseudo depth of the surface as a weighted average of the depth of each sampled point in volume rendering [S3]. Compute the feature in five points around the pseudo surface point by adding small perturbations during training and inference [S3]. When optimizing the scene with CLIPNeRF, backpropagate gradients to only the rays selected by the target text label (e.g., 'apple'), rather than all rays [S3].
+- **Parameter**: Number of feature sampling points: 5 points around the pseudo surface point [S3].
+- **Pitfall**: The method is described as 'very efficient but works for training the distilled feature field', implying it may not be suitable for final inference or high-fidelity rendering compared to full volume rendering [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of specific applications and integrators evolved from the need to address limitations in standard direct volume ray casting, such as the lack of physical lighting accuracy, the difficulty of interactive clipping, and the need for specialized data handling in fields like medical imaging and additive manufacturing. Cinematic Rendering emerged from medical imaging needs for photo-realistic surgical planning [9473-cinematic-rendering.md]. The TVAM integrator addresses the specific physics of light-curing in additive manufacturing [6814-tvam-integrator.md]. The layered approach to two-level volume rendering was proposed to simplify the compositing logic of multi-material volumes [S1]. The Sobierajski/Kaufman framework represents an early comprehensive attempt to integrate global illumination into volume visualization [S2, unofficial].
+The specific applications and integrators described here span a wide range of domains. Customization Volume Integration and Cutting Plane Integration address interactive visualization needs in medical and scientific imaging, allowing users to dynamically adjust appearance and visibility [S1]. TVAM Integrators are specific to additive manufacturing simulation, where the physics of light projection and curing must be modeled [S1]. Cinematic Rendering and Colored Stochastic Shadow Mapping bring photo-realism and global illumination to medical imaging, moving beyond standard DVR [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. Directional Occlusion Shading enhances perceptual cues in interactive rendering [S2, unofficial]. Compositional Rendering and Virtual Magic Lantern address user interaction and scene composition in emerging neural rendering and VR contexts [S1]. The Two Level Volume Rendering with Layers technique offers an alternative compositing strategy for multi-material datasets [S1]. The Sobierajski/Kaufman framework represents an early comprehensive approach to volumetric ray tracing with global illumination [S2, unofficial]. Pseudo-depth Feature Rendering is a recent development in the context of neural radiance fields and feature distillation [S3].
 
